# FlowEnergetic / EXPLOSIVE-FILLED COPPER VESSEL WITH A PARTITION, FLYER-PLATE IGNITED, 2D PLANAR
# (Hydro2, energetic.on = 1, elastic.on = 1)
#
# Test 4.3 of Wallis, Barton & Nikiforakis, Comput. Struct. 254 (2021) 106578, their Fig. 4 (the copper can of
# Schoch, Nordin-Bates & Nikiforakis, JCP 252 (2013) 163), FLYER-PLATE version.  Geometry as drawn in Fig. 4:
#   can 24.6 cm long (x = 7.7 .. 32.3 cm), outer half-height 14 cm, walls 2 cm, a 2 cm copper partition in the
#   middle (x = 19 .. 21 cm): two chambers of idealised explosive, 9.3 cm x 12 cm each;
#   copper flyer plate 2 cm thick, half-height 9.6 cm, in contact with the left wall, moving at 400 m/s;
#   air around; domain 40 cm x 20 cm, symmetry plane on y = 0; 1600 x 800 cells (0.25 mm; the paper uses 0.5 mm,
#   at which the detonation of this explosive runs 15 - 19 % fast here, changelog II.60).
# The impact shock crosses the wall and ignites the first chamber (p > 1e8 Pa); the wave then has to cross the
# partition to ignite the second.  The paper's frames: t = 10, 19.5, 29, 33.5, 54.5 us.
# Copper: ideal plasticity, yield stress 0.07 GPa.  No booster.
# DIFFERENCES FROM THE PAPER:
#   * PLANAR, not cylindrically symmetric (Hydro2 has no axisymmetric source terms).
#   * square corners (the paper's sketch rounds them); the plate thickness (2 cm) is read off the sketch.
#   * explosive, copper equation of state and mixture closure as in input_1D_DetonationCopper;
#     settings of input_2D_CopperCan (sharp copper | explosive boundary, species.order = 3, 0.01 % trace gas).
# Plots:  python3 tests/FlowEnergetic/reference/copper_can_plots.py <run_dir> --times 10,19.5,29,33.5,54.5 --paper
plot_file = ./bin/tests/FlowEnergetic/output_2D_CopperCan_Flyer
